    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d757">

                <pb facs="1032" n="1018" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1018"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.932/14a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-27T20:00:00-04:00">April 27, 1944—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>568. Unless you perceive objection please inform Foreign Minister orally that
                    Department hopes to complete in the not distant future a draft commercial
                        treaty<anchor n="21" xml:id="d757-f1"/> as contemplated by Art. VII of the
                    Extraterritoriality Treaty and discuss orally with him the situs of preliminary
                    conversations and negotiations concerning it. Department believes it
                    advantageous to conduct such conversations and negotiations in Chungking. It is
                    assumed that Foreign Minister would also prefer Chungking but it would appear
                    desirable that he initiate the suggestion. If, however, he does not do so you
                    should at this time indicate our preference for Chungking.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d757-f1" n="21" xml:id="d757-f1-content">
                <p>On April 22, the Department in instruction No. 609 had sent a tentative draft to
                    Ambassador Gauss for his comment (711.9321/29). The Ambassador replied on May 17
                    in despatch No. 2572, giving his observations article by article
                    (711.932/26).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
